# frozen_string_literal: true

# ApplicationMcpTool is your app's base MCP tool, the way ApplicationController is
# your app's base controller. Every tool you expose to the AI subclasses this, so
# the authorization and audit seams you wire here apply to all of them.
#
# This file is APP-OWNED and yours to edit. The rails_mcp gem ships no policy: it
# defines *where* authz and audit happen; you define *what* they are, using your own
# stack (Pundit or otherwise, your audit trail).
#
# As stamped it is FAIL-CLOSED: the authorize seam below raises, so nothing runs
# until you implement your real check. That is intentional — a misconfigured install
# denies rather than exposes.
class ApplicationMcpTool < RailsMcp::Tool
  # === AUTHORIZE SEAM (required) ==========================================
  # Called before every `perform`, with the frozen contract:
  #
  #   user:  the acting staff user (resolved app-side from the bearer token in
  #          your Rack/controller layer, then handed to the gem — the gem never
  #          invents an identity)
  #   args:  the declared args for this call
  #   tool:  the tool instance being invoked
  #
  # Raise to DENY (fail closed); return to PERMIT. Keep the `**` so future context
  # keys stay forward-compatible. Replace the raise below with your real check,
  # e.g. a Pundit policy:
  #
  #   def authorize(user:, args:, tool:, **)
  #     raise RailsMcp::NotAuthorized, "no staff user" if user.nil?
  #     raise RailsMcp::NotAuthorized unless Pundit.policy!(user, tool).invoke?
  #   end
  def authorize(user:, args:, tool:)
    raise RailsMcp::NotAuthorized,
      "ApplicationMcpTool#authorize is not implemented — every call is denied " \
      "until you wire your real authorization check here."
  end

  # === AUDIT SEAM (subscribe app-side) ====================================
  # The gem publishes exactly one ActiveSupport::Notifications event per tool call:
  #
  #   "invoke.rails_mcp"  payload: { user:, tool:, args:, result: OR error: }
  #
  # It persists nothing. Subscribe in config/initializers/rails_mcp.rb and write
  # your own audit row so the log answers "which human ran this" — attributed to
  # `user`, never to a generic AI/bot identity. The payload never carries the bearer
  # token or any credential.
end
